Question -----------  Table: Person

+-------------+---------+
| Column Name | Type    |
+-------------+---------+
| id          | int     |
| email       | varchar |
+-------------+---------+
id is the primary key (column with unique values) for this table.
Each row of this table contains an email. The emails will not contain uppercase letters.
 

Write a solution to report all the duplicate emails. Note that it's guaranteed that the email field is not NULL.

Return the result table in any order.

The result format is in the following example.

 

Example 1:

Input: 
Person table:
+----+---------+
| id | email   |
+----+---------+
| 1  | a@b.com |
| 2  | c@d.com |
| 3  | a@b.com |
+----+---------+
Output: 
+---------+
| Email   |
+---------+
| a@b.com |
+---------+
Explanation: a@b.com is repeated two times.





Solution -------------  select email from person 
                        group by email
                        having count(email) > 1;



Explanation --------------  In This Question We Have A Person Table Wherein We Have Personid And Person's Email Id . Here The Question Is Asking Us To Report All
                            The Duplicate Email-Id's . Now To Do This The First Thing That We Need To Do Is That We Need To Group The Emails Sepeartely With Their
                            Respective Personid's . So We Start Our Query With [ select email from person group by email ] So Here We Have Selected All The Emails
                            From Our Data And We Have Sorted The Emails In Form Of Group In A Way Where We Have Similar One's In A Group . Now Since We Sorted Them 
                            Group Wise Now We Have To Answer The Question To Report All The Duplicates . As We Can See Clearly In Our Data That The Duplicate Email 
                            Is ' a@b.com " So The Query To Get This Particular Output After Segregating The Emails In Form Of Group Will Be [ having count(email) > 1 ]
                            Which Basically Means Count The The Email Which Appears More Than Once In Our Data Basically The Duplicate Email Which In Our Case Is a@b.com 
